findings.description:
The "Subset-evaluable tick" principle promises support for agents "evaluated... at 1/4 or 1/16 rate," and the doc presents the subset parameter as the only interface needed. But the tick integrates every active agent with one fixed timestep (Technical Considerations, "Fixed-timestep integration is essential"). An agent evaluated every 4th tick with that dt moves at a quarter of its true speed.

Fixing this needs one of:
- a per-agent dt or elapsed-ticks array;
- multiple tick calls, one per rate group;
- extrapolation on the server between evaluations, which 300-arch hints at with "interpolated positions." That is extrapolation in practice, and it means the arrays the engine reads hold positions it didn't produce.

Each option changes the tick signature or the stability analysis. This is exactly the interface the document says must be right "from the start," so the decision belongs here.

The doc correctly says neighbor queries dominate cost. It also requires "no Python-level per-agent branching" (GPU section). But it never says what a neighbor query returns:
- variable-length lists per agent;
- flat CSR-style (offsets, indices) arrays;
- a padded fixed-K matrix with a mask;
- pair arrays (i, j, distance).

Only the array formats can be vectorized in NumPy. Variable-length lists force the per-agent Python loop the document forbids. The format also sets the memory ceiling: at the 300-arch figure of ~100 neighbors per agent, 500K agents is about 50M pairs per tick. "Pluggable spatial queries … without changing the engine" holds only if the output format is fixed first. Any later strategy (spatial hash, KD-tree via `scipy.spatial.cKDTree.query_ball_point`, which returns lists of lists) has to produce that format. Name the format and a max-neighbor or cap policy.

Technical Considerations still lists these as open:
- SoA vs AoS ("needs validation");
- Euler vs Verlet vs semi-implicit Euler;
- a separate "Spatial query abstraction" slice that comes after flocking.

Slice 100 (complete) chose NumPy SoA, semi-implicit Euler, and a spatial query interface with a brute-force implementation. The precision question (float64 internally vs Float32 on the wire) is also still open. The doc's own wording, "zero-copy serialization path," is impossible if internal precision is float64, because converting is a copy. The architecture should record the decisions that were made, so later slices don't reopen them.
